Exactly. What you’re pointing at is a local optimization that can create a system-level
distortion.
“Low-hanging fruit” sounds efficient because it maximizes visible throughput per unit time.
But in a piece-work or first-touch-owns-client environment, it can also create a perverse
incentive: workers rationally select the cheapest jobs, accumulate client relationships and
completion counts, and leave the expensive tail for somebody else. The individual looks
productive while the queue gets structurally worse.
That means the backlog doesn’t just have an age or priority. It has a cost gradient.
If the queue contains jobs with efforts , cherry-picking tends to remove the
smallest 's first. So even if the number of items in the backlog is falling, the expected effort
of the remaining item rises:
That is nasty because management dashboards often measure:
when the more meaningful measure is something closer to:
The former rewards churn-n-burn. The latter exposes it.
And yes, once client ownership follows first touch, it becomes more than cherry-picking.
There’s an acquisition incentive embedded in the scheduling rule. Easy jobs are
disproportionately valuable because they give you both:
1. a cheap completion, and
2. potentially a durable client relationship.
So the worker isn't merely optimizing workload. They're optimizing future
revenue/portfolio ownership.
That can absolutely behave like a soft form of client poaching even though nobody is
explicitly “stealing” anything. The queue-selection mechanism itself allocates clients.
The deeper implication for your workflow model is important: FIFO, priority, and “next
available” are not enough dimensions for backlog ordering. You probably need to
represent at least:
