priority, age, effort, deadline, capacity fit, ownership/acquisition eff Then insertion isn't simply “put the next task in the first open slot.” It becomes a constrained
scheduling problem where you deliberately prevent the system from continually exporting
expensive work into the future.
A particularly useful concept here is aging with effort compensation. Instead of letting
easy work always outrank difficult work, the effective priority of a job rises as it waits:
You can tune that so a nasty six-hour job cannot sit there indefinitely while twenty-minute
jobs repeatedly leapfrog it.
There's also a beautiful inversion of the chair game:
Low-hanging fruit maximizes early visible completion.
High-cost-first minimizes the cost of the remaining backlog.
Those are fundamentally different objectives.
And I think the management mistake you're identifying is treating them as though they're
the same thing.
If your objective is this week's closure count, cherry-picking may genuinely win.
If your objective is queue health, predictable capacity, client fairness, and long-run
throughput, it can be disastrous.
There's even a name-adjacent concept from scheduling theory that fits part of this:
Shortest Processing Time (SPT) scheduling. SPT is mathematically excellent for
minimizing average completion/waiting time. But it also has the exact pathology you're
worried about: long jobs can be repeatedly disadvantaged unless you add aging, fairness,
deadlines, or another constraint.
